  "enhanced_information": "The patient likely has narcolepsy, characterized by excessive daytime sleepiness, cataplexy (episodes of paralysis), and hypnagogic hallucinations. The standard treatments for narcolepsy include stimulants (like modafinil) and sodium oxybate. Modafinil is known to cause side effects such as nervousness and, less commonly, hallucinations. Sodium oxybate can also lead to hallucinations but is primarily used to treat cataplexy and improve nighttime sleep. The side effects most closely associated with standard narcolepsy treatments, particularly stimulants, include nervousness and potential hallucinations. Parkinsonism and tardive dyskinesia are not associated with narcolepsy treatments but rather with antipsychotic medications. Therefore, the most relevant side effects from the options provided are nervousness and hallucinations, aligning with the consensus on stimulant use in narcolepsy management."
